{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"privacy-programme-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Small organisation privacy programme track","description":"Sets up a proportionate privacy programme for a small organisation in five gated steps - data inventory, lawful bases and notices, vendors and transfers, rights and breaches, retention and upkeep.","category":"compliance","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","plan","build","operate"],"role":["operations-manager","founder","legal-professional"],"subject":["law"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","document"],"output":["table","plan","checklist","docs"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["legal"],"invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["privacy-programme","data-protection","records-of-processing","privacy-governance","small-business-privacy"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["map-personal-data-processing","run-legitimate-interests-assessment","review-data-processing-agreement","handle-data-subject-request","plan-data-breach-response","write-data-retention-schedule"],"personas":["compliance-officer"]},"args":[{"name":"organisation","description":"The organisation - country, sector, size, customers and staff, what it does with personal data, the main systems and vendors, and anything already in place (privacy notice, policies, contracts).","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"priority","description":"What prompted this - a client questionnaire, a complaint, a funding round, a new product, a regulator letter, or general housekeeping. Optional.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"inventory","file":"steps/01-inventory.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"privacy/01-data-inventory.md"},{"id":"bases","file":"steps/02-bases-and-notices.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"privacy/02-bases-and-notices.md"},{"id":"vendors","file":"steps/03-vendors.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"privacy/03-vendors-and-transfers.md"},{"id":"rights","file":"steps/04-rights-and-breaches.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"privacy/04-rights-and-breaches.md"},{"id":"upkeep","file":"steps/05-retention-and-upkeep.md","stage":"operate","gate":"none","artifact":"privacy/05-retention-and-upkeep.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Builds a privacy programme the size of the organisation, not a binder of templates. Each step produces one artifact and stops for approval; later steps reuse the approved inventory, so gaps found early are fixed once.\n\n<organisation>\n{{organisation}}\n</organisation>\n{{#priority}}Prompted by: {{priority}}{{/priority}}\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n\nRules for every step:\n- Use only facts the person has given or confirmed. Mark assumptions, and keep a running list of open questions.\n- Name the law you assume applies (for example EU or UK GDPR, a US state privacy law, LGPD) and mark deadlines, thresholds and article references \"verify\". Do not cite article numbers the person did not supply.\n- Scale to the organisation: a five-person firm needs a short register and a few procedures, not an enterprise framework.\n- Never recommend deleting or altering records to avoid a request, complaint or investigation.\n- Say early when a data protection professional or lawyer should review: special category or children's data at scale, monitoring or profiling, a live complaint or regulator contact, or international transfers without a clear mechanism.\n- If the person asks to skip a step, say in two lines what depends on it, then do what they ask once they confirm.","steps":[{"id":"inventory","file":"steps/01-inventory.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"privacy/01-data-inventory.md","text":"# Step 1: Data inventory\n\n1. Ask for anything missing that changes the picture: systems and spreadsheets used, vendors, staff and customer counts, countries involved, and any sensitive data (health, children, biometrics, criminal records).\n2. Build the register of processing: one row per activity (for example payroll, customer orders, newsletter, CCTV, recruitment) with purpose, people, data categories, source, systems, vendors, recipients, countries, and a retention placeholder.\n3. Flag high-risk activities that may need a DPIA.\n\nSections: Register, High-risk flags, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval and answers."},{"id":"bases","file":"steps/02-bases-and-notices.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"privacy/02-bases-and-notices.md","text":"# Step 2: Lawful bases and notices\n\n1. For each register row, propose a lawful basis with a one-line reason, and flag rows that need consent or an extra condition (marketing, cookies, special category data).\n2. List legitimate-interest rows that need a written assessment.\n3. Compare the existing privacy notice (if supplied) against the register and list what is missing or wrong; draft the missing sections in plain language.\n\nSections: Bases table, Assessments needed, Notice gaps, Draft notice sections.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"vendors","file":"steps/03-vendors.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"privacy/03-vendors-and-transfers.md","text":"# Step 3: Vendors and transfers\n\n1. From the register, list every vendor that processes personal data, its role (processor or controller), whether a data processing agreement is in place, and where data is stored or accessed from.\n2. Mark transfers that may need a mechanism and assessment, marked \"verify\".\n3. Prioritise vendors by risk and list what to request from each (agreement, security evidence, subprocessor list).\n\nSections: Vendor table, Transfers to resolve, Requests to send.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"rights","file":"steps/04-rights-and-breaches.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"privacy/04-rights-and-breaches.md","text":"# Step 4: Rights requests and breaches\n\n1. Write a one-page procedure for rights requests: how they arrive, who logs them, identity checks, search locations from the register, deadlines to verify, and the reply.\n2. Write a one-page breach procedure: containment, who decides, risk assessment, notification thresholds and deadlines to verify, the breach log, and contacts.\n3. Name owners and backups for both.\n\nSections: Rights procedure, Breach procedure, Owners.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"upkeep","file":"steps/05-retention-and-upkeep.md","stage":"operate","gate":"none","artifact":"privacy/05-retention-and-upkeep.md","text":"# Step 5: Retention and upkeep\n\n1. Fill the register's retention column with proposed periods and triggers, marked \"verify\" where a legal period may apply, and how deletion happens in each system.\n2. Set up upkeep: a short staff training plan, an annual review date, and events that trigger a review (new system, new vendor, new product, a breach).\n3. Summarise the programme on one page: what exists now, what is open, owners, and dates.\n\nSections: Retention, Upkeep plan, Programme summary, Open items."}]}
